WEBVTT

00:00:00.748 --> 00:00:02.498
Nonessential elements.

00:00:02.498 --> 00:00:08.128
Extra information gets a matching pair of marks: commas, dashes, or parentheses.

00:00:09.677 --> 00:00:11.327
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:11.327 --> 00:00:15.202
A comma or a dash already opens a phrase in the sentence.

00:00:15.202 --> 00:00:17.576
And the choices close it in different ways.

00:00:18.593 --> 00:00:23.042
It's testing whether you can see where extra information starts, and where it ends.

00:00:24.102 --> 00:00:25.377
Here's the method.

00:00:25.377 --> 00:00:27.307
Find the extra information.

00:00:27.307 --> 00:00:28.832
Delete it in your head.

00:00:28.832 --> 00:00:30.742
Is the sentence still complete?

00:00:30.742 --> 00:00:32.517
Match the opening mark.

00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:34.617
A dash closes with a dash.

00:00:34.617 --> 00:00:36.742
A comma closes with a comma.

00:00:36.742 --> 00:00:39.575
And a title right before a name takes no commas.

00:00:40.639 --> 00:00:44.614
Rule one: extra information needs a matching pair of marks.

00:00:44.614 --> 00:00:47.564
Here a comma opens it, but nothing closes it.

00:00:47.564 --> 00:00:48.926
Add the second comma.

00:00:50.006 --> 00:00:52.891
Rule two: never mix a dash with a comma.

00:00:52.891 --> 00:00:56.136
Opened with a dash, closed with a comma, is wrong.

00:00:56.136 --> 00:00:57.651
Dash, then dash.

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:02.464
Rule three: a title right before a name gets no commas.

00:01:02.464 --> 00:01:05.114
Not astronaut, comma, Sally Ride.

00:01:05.114 --> 00:01:07.110
Just astronaut Sally Ride.

00:01:08.148 --> 00:01:09.378
Let's try one.

00:01:09.378 --> 00:01:11.428
Here's the extra information.

00:01:11.428 --> 00:01:13.178
It opens with a dash.

00:01:13.178 --> 00:01:17.163
Delete it, and you get: the seed vault stores backup copies.

00:01:17.163 --> 00:01:19.038
Still a complete sentence.

00:01:19.038 --> 00:01:22.138
It opened with a dash, so it must close with a dash.

00:01:22.138 --> 00:01:24.138
A comma mixes the marks.

00:01:24.138 --> 00:01:27.203
A semicolon cuts the subject off from its verb.

00:01:27.203 --> 00:01:28.903
And nothing leaves it open.

00:01:28.903 --> 00:01:30.306
The answer is B.

00:01:31.373 --> 00:01:33.573
Watch out for mismatched marks.

00:01:33.573 --> 00:01:36.183
Opened with a dash, closed with a comma.

00:01:36.183 --> 00:01:37.661
Always match the pair.

00:01:38.711 --> 00:01:40.581
Here's the lesson on one page.

00:01:40.581 --> 00:01:41.641
How to spot it.

00:01:41.641 --> 00:01:42.991
The four steps.

00:01:42.991 --> 00:01:44.096
The rules.

00:01:44.096 --> 00:01:45.121
And the trap.

00:01:45.121 --> 00:01:47.879
Pause the video now, and copy it into your notes.
